From 83614574cfa1fd7e6ad7a3a67f9304e78a4c30fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 22:52:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(credential-pool): persist probe-restored Codex quota
 across reloads [daveey]

A successful Codex /usage probe (#43747) cleared the cooldown in memory with
last_status_at=None. The auth-store recency merge read the missing stamp as
epoch 0, judged the still-future on-disk exhaustion newer, and kept it: the
probing process served the account while disk and every fresh process stayed
exhausted. Reproduced on installed core 73ee883f (hub + mini).

The recovery now keeps the superseded failure generation (the exhaustion's
last_status_at) on the ok row, and the merge lets an ok row stamped at or
after a stale writer's exhaustion win, so:
- a fresh reload reads the restored row as ok;
- a stale writer still holding that same exhaustion cannot write it back;
- a 429 stamped after the recovered generation still binds (disk + reload);
- the same holds through a named profile's root write-through (#100339).

No change to tokens, pins, eligibility, DEAD verdicts, model cooldowns or the
probe itself; a probe that says "still exhausted" clears nothing.

Health ratchet: _merge_disk_cooldown_state and _POOL_STATUS_FIELDS move to
hermes_cli/auth_pool_merge.py (re-exported by hermes_cli.auth);
_normalize_error_context moves to agent/credential_pool_timestamps.py
(re-exported by agent.credential_pool). Moves are verbatim.

Why not caught: the restored-quota test asserted the selecting process's
in-memory entry and token rotation, never a fresh-process reload of disk.

Tests: tests/agent/test_credential_pool_codex_probe_generation.py (3 RED
before, 5 GREEN after; each source half is required by at least one test).
